# Converted to the revised ten-section template (plus section 11); content and tables carried from the previous version.
BOOK = 'Isaiah'
TESTAMENT = 'Old Testament'
GENRE = 'prophets'
SUBTITLE = 'The Holy One of Israel, the Servant, and the Kingdom Promised'
SECTIONS = [(1, 'Book Setting & Program Identity', [('p', 'This section introduces you to the book by explaining who wrote it, who received it, and what was happening when it was written. It also shows you which of God’s programs the book belongs to — Israel’s prophetic program or the Body of Christ’s mystery program. Knowing the setting and the program identity helps you read the book in its proper context and understand how its teachings fit into the larger story of Scripture.'), ('p', 'Isaiah is “The vision of Isaiah the son of Amoz, which he saw concerning Judah and Jerusalem” (Isa. 1:1). It moves from judgment on Judah and the vision of the LORD, through the sign of Immanuel and the child born, to oracles over the nations, the days of Hezekiah, the comfort of Israel, the Servant, and the new heavens and the new earth (Isa. 6:3; 7:14; 9:6; 40:3; 53:5; 65:17).'), ('box', 'Program Identity:', 'This book belongs entirely to the Prophecy Program, where the LORD speaks to Judah and Jerusalem and to Israel about their sin, their judgment, their restoration and their King, with word to the nations beside it (Isa. 1:1; 2:2; 43:1).')]),
(2, 'Narrative Flow of Isaiah', [('p', 'This section gives you a simple walkthrough of how the book unfolds. Instead of diving into details right away, it shows the major movements in the book — how its message begins, how it develops, and how it builds toward its close. Seeing the flow of the book helps you understand the big picture before you study the individual passages.'), ('h3', 'Movement 1 — Judgment and the King Promised (Chs. 1–12)'), ('p', 'The LORD reasons with Judah, Isaiah sees Him on a throne, and the sign and the child and the Branch are promised (Isa. 1:18; 6:3; 7:14; 9:6–7; 11:1).'), ('h3', 'Movement 2 — The Nations and the Day of the LORD (Chs. 13–35)'), ('p', 'Oracles on the nations, the LORD’s counsel, and the opening of the eyes of the blind (Isa. 14:24; 25:8; 26:3; 35:5).'), ('h3', 'Movement 3 — Hezekiah and the Turning Point (Chs. 36–39)'), ('p', 'Jerusalem is threatened and delivered, and Babylon is named for the first time (Isa. 36–39).'), ('h3', 'Movement 4 — Comfort and the Servant (Chs. 40–55)'), ('p', 'The voice in the wilderness, the incomparable LORD, the Servant, and the invitation to the thirsty (Isa. 40:3, 28; 42:1; 49:6; 53:5–6; 55:1).'), ('h3', 'Movement 5 — Glory and the New Heavens (Chs. 56–66)'), ('p', 'The Redeemer comes to Zion, the light arises, and the new heavens and the new earth are made (Isa. 59:20; 60:1; 61:1; 65:17; 66:22).')]),
(3, 'Program Themes in Isaiah', [('p', 'This section highlights the major ideas emphasized in the book — the big doctrinal themes that shape how Israel should think and live under the covenants. These themes are not verse-by-verse explanations; they are the book’s main emphases, the repeated ideas God wants Israel to carry forward. Seeing these themes together helps you understand the book’s message before you look at the individual truths in the tables.'), ('ol', ['<b>The LORD Reasoning With Judah</b> — “Come now, and let us reason together, saith the LORD” (Isa. 1:18).', '<b>The Mountain of the LORD</b> — “It shall come to pass in the last days, that the mountain of the LORD’s house shall be established in the top of the mountains” (Isa. 2:2).', '<b>The Holy One</b> — “Holy, holy, holy, is the LORD of hosts: the whole earth is full of his glory” (Isa. 6:3).', '<b>The Throne of David</b> — “Of the increase of his government and peace there shall be no end, upon the throne of David, and upon his kingdom” (Isa. 9:7).', '<b>The Remnant Recovered</b> — “The Lord shall set his hand again the second time to recover the remnant of his people” (Isa. 11:11).', '<b>The Servant</b> — “Behold my servant, whom I uphold; mine elect, in whom my soul delighteth” (Isa. 42:1).', '<b>The New Heavens and the New Earth</b> — “I create new heavens and a new earth” (Isa. 65:17).'])]),
(4, 'Christ in Isaiah', [('p', 'This section gathers what the book teaches about Christ — through foreshadowing, typology, and prophetic anticipation. These statements help you see the book’s Christ-centered trajectory before you study the doctrinal truths in detail. By collecting these points together, you get a clear picture of how Isaiah points to Christ.'), ('ol', ['<b>The Sign</b> — “A virgin shall conceive, and bear a son, and shall call his name Immanuel” (Isa. 7:14) is quoted in the Gospel of Matthew (Matt. 1:22–23).', '<b>The Child Born</b> — “Unto us a child is born, unto us a son is given” (Isa. 9:6).', '<b>The Voice in the Wilderness</b> — “The voice of him that crieth in the wilderness, Prepare ye the way of the LORD” (Isa. 40:3) is quoted of John the Baptist (Matt. 3:3).', '<b>The Suffering Servant</b> — “He was wounded for our transgressions” (Isa. 53:5) and “the LORD hath laid on him the iniquity of us all” (Isa. 53:6) are the verses the New Testament takes up (Acts 8:32–35; 1 Pet. 2:24–25).', '<b>The Anointed One</b> — “The Spirit of the Lord GOD is upon me” (Isa. 61:1) is the passage the Lord reads at Nazareth (Luke 4:16–21).'])]),
(5, 'Israel, the Law, and the Covenants in Isaiah', [('p', 'This section helps you see how Isaiah speaks about Israel, the law, and the covenants without mixing programs. Isaiah operates within Israel’s prophetic framework. By gathering these points together, this section shows you how to read Isaiah in its own setting — as an Israel-program book — while recognizing what it says (and does not say) about the Body of Christ.'), ('p', 'Isaiah reinforces Israel’s:'), ('ul', ['Judah and Jerusalem as the LORD’s people addressed (Isa. 1:1)', 'the mountain of the LORD’s house and the nations coming to it (Isa. 2:2)', 'the throne of David and a kingdom without end (Isa. 9:7)', 'the remnant of his people recovered (Isa. 11:11)', 'the Redeemer coming to Zion (Isa. 59:20)']), ('p', 'All content belongs exclusively to the Prophecy Program.')]),
(6, 'Israel-Program Distinctions', [('p', 'This section highlights the teachings in Isaiah that belong uniquely to Israel’s prophetic program — truths rooted in prophecy, covenant promises, national identity, and God’s dealings with Israel. By gathering these together, this section helps you see how Isaiah contributes to Israel’s story without borrowing from the Body-of-Christ program.'), ('ul', ['Concerning Judah and Jerusalem — “which he saw concerning Judah and Jerusalem.” (Isa. 1:1).', 'The Mountain of the LORD — “the mountain of the LORD’s house shall be established in the top of the mountains.” (Isa. 2:2).', 'The Sign of Immanuel — “a virgin shall conceive, and bear a son.” (Isa. 7:14).', 'The Throne of David — “upon the throne of David, and upon his kingdom, to order it.” (Isa. 9:7).', 'The Branch — “there shall come forth a rod out of the stem of Jesse.” (Isa. 11:1).', 'The Remnant Recovered — “recover the remnant of his people.” (Isa. 11:11).', 'Jacob and Israel Redeemed — “I have redeemed thee, I have called thee by thy name; thou art mine.” (Isa. 43:1).', 'The Preserved of Israel — “to restore the preserved of Israel.” (Isa. 49:6).', 'The Redeemer Comes to Zion — “the Redeemer shall come to Zion.” (Isa. 59:20).', 'The Anointed Servant — “The Spirit of the Lord GOD is upon me.” (Isa. 61:1).', 'The Seed Remaining — “so shall your seed and your name remain.” (Isa. 66:22).'])]),
(7, 'Truths Table: What Isaiah Says', [('p', 'This table gathers the key statements, instructions, and teachings in Isaiah and shows which ones belong to Israel’s prophetic program. Each row lists a truth from the book, explains what it says, and identifies its program identity based on Israel’s covenant setting. This helps you see, at a glance, what Isaiah teaches directly to Israel and keeps those truths separate from universal principles that apply more broadly.'), ('table', ['Category', 'Truth Revealed in Isaiah', 'Program Identity', 'Scripture'], [['Concerning Judah and Jerusalem', '“which he saw concerning Judah and Jerusalem.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Isa. 1:1'], ['The Mountain of the LORD', '“the mountain of the LORD’s house shall be established in the top of the mountains.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Isa. 2:2'], ['The Sign of Immanuel', '“a virgin shall conceive, and bear a son.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Isa. 7:14'], ['The Throne of David', '“upon the throne of David, and upon his kingdom, to order it.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Isa. 9:7'], ['The Branch', '“there shall come forth a rod out of the stem of Jesse.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Isa. 11:1'], ['The Remnant Recovered', '“recover the remnant of his people.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Isa. 11:11'], ['Jacob and Israel Redeemed', '“I have redeemed thee, I have called thee by thy name; thou art mine.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Isa. 43:1'], ['The Preserved of Israel', '“to restore the preserved of Israel.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Isa. 49:6'], ['The Redeemer Comes to Zion', '“the Redeemer shall come to Zion.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Isa. 59:20'], ['The Anointed Servant', '“The Spirit of the Lord GOD is upon me.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Isa. 61:1'], ['The Seed Remaining', '“so shall your seed and your name remain.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Isa. 66:22']], [20, 34, 26, 20])]),
(8, 'Universal Truths Table (All People, All Time)', [('p', 'This table gathers the truths in Isaiah that apply to everyone, not just Israel. These are principles rooted in God’s character — things that hold true across all programs and all ages. By separating these universal truths from Israel’s covenant-specific instructions, this section helps you see what Isaiah teaches all believers everywhere and what it teaches Israel in its own setting.'), ('table', ['Universal Truth', 'Description', 'Scripture'], [['The Holy LORD', '“Holy, holy, holy, is the LORD of hosts: the whole earth is full of his glory.”', 'Isa. 6:3'], ['Perfect Peace', '“Thou wilt keep him in perfect peace, whose mind is stayed on thee.”', 'Isa. 26:3'], ['The Word Stands', '“the word of our God shall stand for ever.”', 'Isa. 40:8'], ['The Creator Faints Not', '“the everlasting God, the LORD, the Creator of the ends of the earth, fainteth not, neither is weary.”', 'Isa. 40:28'], ['The First and the Last', '“I am the first, and I am the last; and beside me there is no God.”', 'Isa. 44:6'], ['Look and Be Saved', '“Look unto me, and be ye saved, all the ends of the earth: for I am God, and there is none else.”', 'Isa. 45:22'], ['The End From the Beginning', '“Declaring the end from the beginning.”', 'Isa. 46:10'], ['All Have Gone Astray', '“All we like sheep have gone astray.”', 'Isa. 53:6'], ['His Thoughts and Ways', '“my thoughts are not your thoughts, neither are your ways my ways.”', 'Isa. 55:8']], [28, 48, 24])]),
(9, 'Program-Safe Application Grid', [('p', 'This section shows how to apply the teachings of Isaiah without mixing programs. The applications come from Israel’s covenant story and from universal truths that apply to everyone. By keeping the applications tied to what the book actually says — and not borrowing commands from Israel’s covenants for the Body of Christ — this grid helps you respond to Isaiah in a way that is faithful to its setting, clear, and safe.'), ('ol', ['<b>Revere His holiness</b> — Holy, holy, holy (Isa. 6:3).', '<b>Keep the mind on Him</b> — Perfect peace (Isa. 26:3).', '<b>Trust the word</b> — It shall stand for ever (Isa. 40:8).', '<b>Rest in the Creator</b> — He fainteth not (Isa. 40:28).', '<b>Know there is no other God</b> — The first and the last (Isa. 44:6).', '<b>Look to Him</b> — Look unto me, and be ye saved (Isa. 45:22).', '<b>Admit the common need</b> — All have gone astray (Isa. 53:6).', '<b>Receive His higher ways</b> — His thoughts are not ours (Isa. 55:8).'])]),
(10, 'Teaching Outline for Isaiah', [('p', 'This outline gives you a teacher-ready structure for presenting the book. It follows the narrative movements and highlights the major themes, making it easy to teach Isaiah in classes, small groups, or personal study.'), ('olr', ['Judgment and the King Promised (Chs. 1–12) — Vision, sign and child. <i>(Isa. 1:18; 6:3; 7:14; 9:6–7; 11:1)</i>', 'The Nations and the Day of the LORD (Chs. 13–35) — Counsel and peace. <i>(Isa. 14:24; 26:3; 35:5)</i>', 'Hezekiah and the Turning Point (Chs. 36–39) — Deliverance and Babylon. <i>(Isa. 36–39)</i>', 'Comfort and the Servant (Chs. 40–55) — The incomparable LORD. <i>(Isa. 40:3, 8, 28; 44:6; 53:5–6; 55:8)</i>', 'Glory and the New Heavens (Chs. 56–66) — The Redeemer and the new creation. <i>(Isa. 59:20; 61:1; 65:17; 66:22)</i>'])]),
(11, 'Companion Usage and Study Suggestions', [('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Personal Study'), ('p', 'This Companion is designed to walk alongside you as you read Isaiah, helping you see both the prophetic structure of the book and the timeless truths rooted in God’s character. Use it slowly, chapter by chapter, letting the narrative movements, themes, and universal truths deepen your understanding. The Program-Safe Applications will guide your heart toward worship, trust, and attention to the word of the LORD without confusing Israel’s covenant commands with the Body of Christ’s calling today.'), ('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Small Groups or Classes'), ('p', 'This Companion provides a clear, structured framework that makes teaching Isaiah simple and accessible. Each section gives you ready-to-use material: narrative movements for overview, prophecy-program themes for doctrinal clarity, universal truths for discussion, and a pastoral teaching outline for weekly lessons. Use the tables to help your group distinguish Israel’s covenant identity from universal principles, and use the Program-Safe Application Grid to foster meaningful, Christ-centered conversation.'), ('h3', 'How This Companion Fits Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series'), ('p', 'This Companion is part of a larger series designed to help believers read every book of Scripture without blurring God’s prophetic program for Israel and His mystery program for the Body of Christ. Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series, Isaiah stands firmly in Israel’s covenant story, yet it still reveals universal truths that shape Christian character today. This Companion shows you how to honor the book’s prophetic context while drawing out the timeless principles that apply to all believers in every age.')])]

# Place in God's Storyline (Master Standard v1.155): last item of Section 1
from storyline import S as _STORY
SECTIONS[0][2].append(('p', '<b>Place in God’s Storyline.</b> ' + _STORY['isaiah'][1]))
